-12-18T13:29:00-08:00","id":1527065,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1526937-how-often-do-you-use-historical-fiction-as-inspiration?p=1526953#post1526953\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you mean in terms of backgrounds for characters? It depends. I read a lot of historical fiction. I like James Clavell, Alan Furst, Bernard Cornwell, and Steven Saylor. It can help with creating NPCs.<br /> <br /> If running a chronicle, I read a lot of history of the city or region I am setting the chronicle in.</div> </div> </div> </div>Steven Saylor is amazing. The Gordianus series is great. James Clavell and Bernard Cornwell are good too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}